---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import LessonNav from '../../components/LessonNav.astro';
import LessonEyebrow from '../../components/LessonEyebrow.astro';
import { rules, umbrella, ruleCount, ruleCountWord, ruleCountWordCap } from '../../data/rules.js';

// Lesson 1 of the whole course, and the only page on the site that deliberately
// carries NO outbound links.
//
// It replaces /principles, which was lesson 5 of 101 and linked out to ten other
// pages — seven of them further along the course. That made it a table of contents
// wearing a lesson's clothes, and it was the most anti-linear thing here.
//
// Moving it to the FRONT is what lets the links go. At position 1 a link forward
// would be a link to material the reader hasn't got to; instead each rule names its
// level and its exact lesson title in plain text, so it's findable in the menu
// without pulling anyone out of the sequence. The lessons then link BACK here (see
// components/RuleBand.astro). Links point backwards.
//
// FLATTENED 2026-07-29 (later): the rules used to render grouped under 101/102/103
// /104 level headings. That reimposed the course's structure on a page whose whole
// job is to be the short version — you had to parse five groups before you could
// read twelve lines. It is now one unbroken numbered list. The level still appears,
// once, in each rule's "in full" pointer, which is where it earns its place.
//
// The primer exists because a reader arriving cold does not yet know what a seed
// phrase is, and rule 06 is meaningless without it. Three sentences buys the whole
// page its vocabulary.
---
<Base
  title={`The ${ruleCount} rules of holding your own Bitcoin · BitcoinKeys.guide`}
  description={`The whole guide, compressed into ${ruleCount} plain-English rules. Follow them and your Bitcoin is safe. Every lesson that follows is one of these rules in full.`}
  verified="2026-07-29"
>
  <section class="prose">
    <LessonEyebrow />
    <h1>{ruleCountWordCap} rules. <span class="accent">That’s the whole thing.</span></h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Everything on this site compresses into the rules below. Follow them and your Bitcoin is safe —
      genuinely, that is the entire deal. You don’t have to understand all of them yet. That’s what the
      rest of the course is for: every lesson after this one is a single rule, in full.
    </p>
    <hr class="lead-sep" />

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="label">A few words before you start</span>
      <p style="margin:0">
        Your Bitcoin is controlled by a secret <strong>key</strong>. That key is written down as a list of
        12 or 24 ordinary words — your <strong>seed phrase</strong>, and whoever has those words has the
        money. A <strong>hardware wallet</strong> is a small dedicated gadget that keeps the key offline and
        signs payments without ever handing it over. That’s enough vocabulary for every rule below.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <ol class="rules">
      {rules.map((r) => (
        <li class="rule" id={`rule-${r.n}`}>
          <span class="r-n">{String(r.n).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <div class="r-body">
            <h2>{r.rule}</h2>
            <p set:html={r.body}></p>
            <p class="r-where">In full: <span class="mono">{r.level}</span> · {r.lesson}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <div class="callout safe umb">
      <span class="label">And the one that contains all {ruleCountWord}</span>
      <h3>{umbrella.rule}</h3>
      <p set:html={umbrella.body}></p>
      <p class="r-where">In full: <span class="mono">{umbrella.level}</span> · {umbrella.lesson} — optional, and last.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="plain">What to do with this page</h2>
    <p>
      Don’t try to memorise it. Read it once now so the shape of the thing is in your head, then carry on
      to the next lesson — the rules will make far more sense once you’ve met the ideas behind them. Come
      back here whenever you want the whole guide in two minutes, and use it as a checklist before you move
      any meaningful amount of Bitcoin.
    </p>
    <p class="sub">
      Every lesson from here on opens by naming the rule it teaches, so you always know which of the {ruleCountWord}
      you’re working on.
    </p>
  </section>

  <LessonNav />
</Base>

<style>
  .lead-sep { border: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line-soft); max-width: 46ch; margin: 1.4rem 0 1.6rem; }

  .rules { list-style: none; margin: 1.6rem 0 0; padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.7rem; counter-reset: none; }
  .rule { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 3rem 1fr; gap: 1.1rem; align-items: start;
    background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 1.1rem 1.3rem; scroll-margin-top: 5rem; }
  .rule:target { border-color: var(--accent); }
  .r-n { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 1.4rem; font-weight: 800; color: var(--accent); line-height: 1; }
  /* h2 inside the list: these ARE the page's structure now that the level
     headings are gone, so they carry the heading semantics rather than h3. */
  .r-body h2 { margin: 0.05rem 0 0.4rem; color: var(--text); font-size: 1.08rem; letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    border: 0; padding: 0; }
  .r-body p { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .r-where { margin-top: 0.6rem !important; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.73rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em; color: var(--faint) !important; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .r-where .mono { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; }

  .umb { margin-top: 2.4rem; }
  .umb h3 { margin: 0.2rem 0 0.4rem; font-size: 1.14rem; }
  .plain { margin-top: 2.2rem; }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .rule { grid-template-columns: 2.3rem 1fr; gap: 0.8rem; padding: 1rem 1.05rem; }
    .r-n { font-size: 1.2rem; }
    .r-body h2 { font-size: 1.02rem; }
  }
</style>
